In France, voting has traditionally been conducted using basic technology: voters stood in individual booths and placed a pre-printed paper ballot, indicating their chosen candidate, into an envelope.
After the voter's identity is verified by officials, the voter places the ballot paper into the ballot box and signs their name on the voting list.
French election law imposes relatively strict regulations on these practices.
Since 1988, ballot boxes must be transparent, allowing voters and observers to verify that no envelopes are present in the box at the beginning of voting, and that no envelopes other than those of eligible voters are added afterward.
Candidates may appoint representatives to observe each stage of the process. At night, votes are counted by volunteers following specific procedures, under strict supervision.
