Under this group are medium-sized cat-like animals that feed on medium-sized prey, ranging from rabbits to mountain goats and deer.
Finally, many small cats (including lazy domestic cats) eat a large number of small prey such as insects, rodents, lizards, and birds.
Their secret to success is the concept of "kennel," a special practice that every cat breeder performs to keep it separate from competition with others.
Lions are the most social cat-like animals and live in large groups called prides.
Prides consist of one to three related adult males along with about thirty females and their offspring.
Women usually have a close relationship with each other and are part of a large family of sisters and daughters.
The lion groups behave very much like wolves or dogs—animals that, in their behavior, are remarkably similar to lions (but not other large cats)—and are extremely deadly to their prey.
The bear, like a six-pack athlete, can climb (although not very well in this regard), swim, cover long distances, and pull something with five times the strength of a strong human.
The tiger, like the lion, leopard, and jaguar, belongs to the same genus (Panthera). These four species are the only felids capable of roaring.
The roar of a bear is not like the full roar of a lion; rather, it is more similar to a series of grumbling words.
Cubs like to eat small animals. If they can, they hunt monkeys, snakes, rodents, and birds. Almost all the animals that cubs hunt are considerably smaller in size than themselves.
Scientists believe that the American puma tracks its prey by using its sense of smell, locating them (as food) by following their scent trail on the ground.
They can see well in the dark due to their night vision ability, and they can move very quietly. The American wild cat (ocelot) conceals itself in the surrounding environment before hunting prey, then suddenly dives onto its prey.
When a small group of living organisms (a small community) separates from the main community they belong to (for example, when they migrate to the other side of a mountain range or river, or when they move to a new island from which they cannot easily return), they often find themselves in an environment different from their previous one.
This new environment has different resources and competitors, so a new population seeking to become a strong competitor will require different abilities or adaptations beyond what they previously needed.
The original population has not changed in any way; they still require the same adaptations to the environment as before.
Over time, as the new population gradually adapts to the new environment, they become increasingly unlike other people.
Eventually, after thousands or even millions of years, the two populations will be so different that they cannot be considered part of the same species.
We call this process speciation, which simply means the formation of new species. Speciation is an inevitable outcome and a crucial part of evolution.
Plants produce the oxygen that humans breathe in, and take in the carbon dioxide that humans exhale (i.e., breathe out).
Plants produce their own food through photosynthesis. They also provide shade.
We make our homes and clothing from plants. Most of the foods we eat are plant-based. Without plants, animals cannot survive.
