Within just two weeks, the Americans and French forces successfully liberated southern France and turned their advance toward Germany.
A civilization is a unified culture observed by a very large group of people, a society, who live and work together.
The word "civilization" is derived from the Latin word "civilis," meaning civil, which relates to the Latin word "civis," meaning citizen, and "civitas," meaning city, and to some extent also defines the size of a society.
Urban states are leading countries. A civilized culture refers to the transfer of knowledge across several generations, a long cultural footprint, and equitable dissemination.
Minor cultures often disappear without leaving behind relevant historical evidence and cannot be regarded as worthy civilizations.
During the Revolutionary War, the first thirteen states formed a weak central government under the principles of the Confederation—Congress being its only component.
The Congress lacked any power to impose taxes, as there was neither a national executive nor a national judiciary, and it had to rely on state officials for enforcing all its actions—officials who often did not cooperate.
Also, there was no authority to repeal tax and tariff laws between countries.
Before amending these provisions, unanimous agreement from all countries was required, and countries also had to obtain their central governments' approval, even in the absence of a representative.
