Winter sports are most popular in northern regions, and Italians compete in international competitions and Olympic events.
Japan has nearly 7,000 islands (the largest of which is Honshu), making Japan the seventh-largest island country in the world!
Due to its group of islands, Japan is often referred to geographically as an "archipelago."
Taiwan's history dates back to the 15th century, when European navigators passing by named the island "Ilha Formosa," meaning "Beautiful Island."
In 1624, the Dutch East India Company established a base in southwestern Taiwan, marking the beginning of a transformation in local grain production, and employing Chinese workers in their sugar and rice plantations.
In 1683, the Qing dynasty (1644–1912) forcibly took control of western and northern Taiwan, and in 1885 declared Taiwan as one of the provinces of the Qing Empire.
In 1895, the Qing government, after its defeat in the First Sino-Japanese War (1894–1895), signed the Treaty of Shimonoseki, under which control of Taiwan was ceded to Japan, and Japan retained sovereignty over the island until 1945.
The city of Machu Picchu consists of three main structures: Intihuatana, the Temple of the Sun, and the Room of the Three Windows.
Many of the buildings surrounding this complex have been restored to give tourists a better idea of how they originally looked.
By 1976, 30 percent of Machu Picchu had been restored, and restoration work continues to this day.
For example, the most common photographic image format worldwide is 35mm, which was the dominant film size during the late analog film era.
