Ancient China had a unique way of representing different time periods; each time period of China or each family that held power was a distinct dynasty.
Also, between each dynasty, there was an unstable era when provinces were divided. The most famous period was the Three Kingdoms period, which lasted for 60 years between the Han and Jin dynasties.
During this period, fierce wars broke out among many nobles who were fighting for the crown.
These three kingdoms are considered one of the bloodiest periods in ancient Chinese history, during which thousands of people lost their lives in the struggle to seize the highest throne in the great palace in Xi'an.
There are many social and political effects, including the adoption of the metric system, the shift from absolutism to republicanism, nationalism, and the belief that the country belongs to the people, not to a single ruler.
