Of course, the extraordinary profits derived from the long-term exploitation of the Filipino people constitute the fundamental achievements of American imperialism.
To understand the Templars, you must understand the context that led to the creation of this order.
The era in which these events occurred is generally called the Middle Ages, a period in European history during the 11th, 12th, and 13th centuries (1000 to 1300 AD).
The High Middle Ages followed the Middle Ages, and was succeeded by the Late Middle Ages, which conventionally ends around 1500.
Technological determinism is a term that actually encompasses a wide spectrum of beliefs, ranging from the idea of technological pressure or technological necessity to a more precise meaning suggesting that an inherent logic related to scientific laws and their manifestation in technology governs human destiny.
Most interpretations of technological determinism share two general ideas: that technological development follows a path beyond the influence of culture or politics, and that technology itself exerts greater "impacts" on inherent societies than on conditional societies.
For example, someone might say that cars necessarily led to the development of roads.
But a national highway network is economically unacceptable for a small number of cars; therefore, in order to reduce car ownership costs, new production methods were developed.
Public ownership of vehicles also leads to a high incidence of road accidents, which in turn results in the development of new methods in healthcare for repairing damaged vehicle bodies.
Romanticism had a strong element of cultural determinism, originating from writers such as "Goethe," "Fichte," and "Schlegel."
In the context of romanticism, geography shaped individuals, and over time, customs and culture related to that geography emerged, aligning with society's position, which was better than the laws arbitrarily imposed by despots.
Just as Paris is known as the capital of modern fashion, Constantinople was regarded as the capital of fashion in feudal Europe.
Its fame began due to its status as an aristocratic center around the year 400 AD and continued until approximately the year 1100 AD.
During the 12th century, its position weakened because the Crusaders returned their goods—such as silk and spices, which were worth more than the goods available in the Eastern Roman market.
At the same time, the transfer of the title of capital of fashion from Constantinople to Paris took place.
Gothic style reached its peak between the 10th–11th centuries and the 14th century.
Initially, clothing was mostly influenced by Byzantine culture in the East.
However, due to the slow communication channels, styles in the West might be 25 to 30 years behind.
In the late Middle Ages, Western Europe began developing fashion. One of the greatest transformations of that time resulted from the Crusades, and people started using buttons to fasten their clothing.
Subsistence farming is agriculture carried out solely to produce enough food to meet the needs of the farmer and his family.
Subsistence agriculture is a simple system, often organic, that uses locally stored seeds from specific ecosystems along with crop rotation or other relatively simple methods to maximize yield.
Historically, most farmers earned a living through agriculture, and this still exists in many developing countries.
Subcultures bring together like-minded individuals who feel neglected by societal standards and help them develop a sense of identity.
Subcultures can differ based on the age, ethnicity, social class, place of residence, and/or gender of their members.
The characteristics that distinguish a subculture may be linguistic, aesthetic, religious, political, sexual, geographical, or a combination of these characteristics.
Members of a subculture often express their membership through a distinctive and symbolic use of style, including fashion, mannerisms, and deliberate speech patterns.
One of the most common methods used to illustrate the importance of socialization is referring to the tragic cases of children who, due to neglect, bad luck, or intentional abuse by adults during their development, failed to learn socialization.
These children are referred to as "wild children." Some of these wild children are confined by people (usually their own parents); in some cases, the abandonment of the child results from parents' denial of the child's severe mental and physical disabilities.
Wild children may have experienced severe abuse or emotional trauma before running away or being abandoned.
It is claimed that the rest were raised by animals; it has been said that some lived alone in the wild.
If raised completely by animals other than humans, a wild child exhibits behaviors (within physical limits) almost identical to those of animals, such as fear and indifference toward humans.
Although project-based learning should make learning easier and more interesting, scaffolding goes a step further.
Scaffolding is not a learning method in itself, but rather support provided to individuals who are experiencing new learning, such as using a new computer program or starting a new project.
Scaffolds may be virtual or physical; in other words, a teacher is a type of scaffold, but a small paper clip in Microsoft Office is also a scaffold.
Virtual scaffolds are embedded within the software, with the aim of prompting, questioning, and explaining methods that might otherwise be challenging for students to handle independently.
Children are placed in foster homes for numerous reasons. The range of these reasons extends from neglect to abuse, and even exploitation.
No child should be forced to grow up in an environment lacking nutrition, care, and education, but this is happening.
We consider the care system for homeless children to be a safe area for these children.
Our care and upbringing system strives to provide safe homes, compassionate caregivers, sustainable education, and reliable healthcare services.
For foster parenting, it is essential that all unmet needs of the child from their previous home be fulfilled.
The Internet combines elements of interpersonal and collective communication.
The distinctive features of the internet have led to additional dimensions in terms of user approach and satisfaction.
For example, "learning" and "social participation" are mentioned as important motivations for using the Internet (James and Sayer, 1995).
"Personal conflict" and "ongoing relationships" were also identified by Eime and McCord (1998) as new motivational aspects when they investigated audience responses to websites.
The use of video recording led to significant discoveries in interpreting microexpressions and facial movements that last only a few milliseconds.
In particular, it is claimed that one can detect when a person is lying by correctly interpreting small expressions.
Oliver Sacks, in his article "The President's Speech," demonstrated how individuals who, due to brain injury, are unable to comprehend speech, are still capable of accurately assessing sincerity.
He even suggests that such abilities could be shared in domestic animals like dogs in interpreting human behavior.
Research in the 20th century showed that there are two sources of genetic changes: hidden and expressed.
Mutation increases genetic diversity and selects it from the reservoir of expressed diversity.
The separation and recombination of differences between the two pools moves the variation back and forth across generations.
Outside the savanna, meeting the amino acid-based nutritional needs from available plant sources has been a challenging task for a primate with a digestive system similar to humans.
Moreover, failing to do so has serious consequences: stunted growth, malnutrition, and ultimately death.
The most accessible plant sources of protein are found in leaves and legumes, but these are difficult for mammals like us to digest unless they are cooked.
In contrast, animal foods (ants, lizards, eggs) are not only easily digestible but also provide high-quality proteins that contain all essential amino acids.
Everything has been taken into account, and if our ancestors nearly solved their "protein problem" in the same way that chimpanzees do today in the savanna, we should not be surprised.
Sleep disorder is the process of becoming consciously aware during normal sleep and then falling back asleep again after a period of time (10-60 minutes).
This can easily be done using a relatively quiet alarm clock that will alert you without fully waking you from sleep.
If you realize you're turning off your alarm clock while still asleep, you can place it on the other side of the room so that you'll be forced to get out of bed to turn it off.
Other circadian rhythm-based options include drinking a large amount of fluids (especially water or tea, a specific diuretic) before bedtime, which forces the individual to wake up from sleep to urinate.
A person's level of inner calm is inversely related to their level of tension in body and mind.
The less stress there is, the more positive the life force gift becomes. Each person has the ability to find absolute peace and satisfaction.
Anyone can attain enlightenment. The only thing that stands in the way of this goal is our tension and negativity.
Tibetan Buddhism is based on the teachings of Buddha, but it has been developed through the Mahayana path of love and numerous techniques from Indian yoga.
In essence, Tibetan Buddhism is very simple, and includes kundalini yoga, mindfulness, and the path of universal love.
Using Kundalini yoga, the Kundalini energy (the energy of enlightenment) is awakened through yoga postures, breathing exercises, mantras, and visualization.
The center of Tibetan meditation is the yoga of the gods. Through visualizing various deities, energy channels are purified, chakras are activated, and enlightened awareness arises.
Germany was a common enemy in World War II, which led to an alliance between the Soviet Union and the United States. After the war, differences in system, process, and culture led to the separation of these countries.
Due to the last two years of the war, former allies had become enemies, and the Cold War began.
It was meant to endure for the next 40 years and to actually fight, alongside proxy armies, on battlefronts stretching from Africa to Asia—in Afghanistan, Cuba, and many other regions.
By September 17, 1939, Poland's defense had collapsed, and the only remaining hope was retreating and reorganizing along the Romanian corridor.
But these plans nearly collapsed when more than 800,000 soldiers from the Red Army of the Soviet Union entered and, after advancing into eastern Poland, established the Belorussian and Ukrainian fronts, thereby violating the Treaty of Riga, the non-aggression pact between the Soviet Union and Poland, and other bilateral and multilateral international agreements.
Using ships for transporting goods is by far the most efficient way to move large numbers of people and cargo across oceans.
Traditionally, the role of naval forces was to ensure that your country could transport its people and goods, while simultaneously interfering with the enemy's ability to move their people and supplies.
One example worth mentioning is the battle in the North Atlantic during World War II. The Americans sought to transport people and supplies across the Atlantic Ocean to assist Britain.
Meanwhile, the German Navy primarily attempted to halt this movement using U-boats.
If Germany had defeated the Allies, it might have been able to conquer Britain, since it already controlled the rest of Europe.
It appears that goats were first domesticated around 10,000 years ago in the Zagros Mountains of Iran.
Ancient cultures and tribes began keeping them for easy access to milk, wool, meat, and skin.
Domestic goats were generally kept in herds on hills or other grazing areas and were usually cared for by goat herders, who were often children or teenagers, similar to the way ordinary shepherds tend their flocks. These herding practices are still in use today.
The wagon line was built in England in the 16th century.
Although the wagon paths consisted only of parallel wooden planks, they allowed horses to pull them to achieve higher speeds and enabled them to haul heavier loads compared to dragging cargo over the rougher roads of that time.
The rail fastener was introduced almost from the very beginning to keep the rails in place. Nevertheless, gradually it was realized that if an iron clip were placed on the rail fastener, the rails would perform more effectively.
Although this practice became common, iron caused increased wear on the wooden wheels of the wagons.
Eventually, wooden wheels were replaced by iron wheels. In 1767, the first fully iron rails were introduced.
The first form of transportation was walking; humans began walking upright about two million years ago with the emergence of upright humans (Homo Erectus).
Their ancestors were the Southerners, who, by custom, did not walk normally.
Bipedal specializations in southern African fossils date back to 4.2–3.9 million years ago, although it's possible that Chad's hominins were already walking on two legs as early as seven million years ago.
We can begin a more friendly life with the environment, join the environmental movement, and even become environmental activists to somewhat reduce future hardships.
This is exactly like many other cases of supportive treatment. Nevertheless, if we are not merely seeking a temporary solution, we must identify the root of the problems and neutralize them.
It is clear that the world has changed due to human scientific and technological advancements, and problems have increased because of the extremely large population and humans' strange and unusual lifestyles.
After its passage by Congress on July 4th, a handwritten draft, signed by John Hancock, President of Congress, and Charles Thomson, Clerk, was sent to John Dunlap's printing office, located a few blocks away.
Between 150 and 200 copies were produced during the night, now known as the "Dunlap Declaration."
John Nixon was the one who read the document for the first time on July 8th in front of the public in the courtyard of the "Independence Hall."
On July 6, a copy was sent to George Washington, who ordered it read to his soldiers in New York on July 9. A copy reached London on August 10.
The Dunlap Plateau 25 still exists and represents the oldest surviving version of this document. The original manuscript has not survived.
Many modern paleontologists believe that a group of dinosaurs survived and are alive today. We call them birds.
Since they have feathers and can fly, many people do not recognize them as dinosaurs.
But there are many things about birds that still resemble dinosaurs.
They have spiky legs and claws, lay eggs, and walk like a stork on their two hind legs.
Almost all computers currently in use are based on the processing of information that is encoded as binary numbers.
A binary digit can only have one of two values, namely 0 or 1, and in computer terminology, these numbers are referred to as binary digits—or bits.
Internal poisoning is not immediately apparent. Symptoms such as vomiting are so common that it cannot be diagnosed immediately.
The most likely cause of internal poisoning could be the presence of an empty medicine container or toxic household chemicals.
Check the label for specific first aid instructions related to that particular poison.
Entomologists use the term "sasa" formally to refer to this group of insects.
This term originates from ancient familiarity with bed bugs, which are highly adaptable insects capable of parasitizing humans.
Both the killer insect and the bed bug are nest-dwelling, meaning they adapt themselves to living in nests or residing within a host.
Across the United States, nearly 400,000 cases of multiple sclerosis (MS) are known, a significant neurological disease in younger and older adults.
MS is a type of disease that affects the central nervous system, which consists of the brain, spinal cord, and optic nerve.
Research has shown that women are twice as likely to develop MS as men.
A couple might decide that raising a newborn is not beneficial for them or their child.
These couples can arrange an adoption plan for their newborn.
In adoption, parents terminate their parental rights so that another couple can care for the child.
The main goal of science is to discover how the world works through the scientific method. This method actually guides most scientific research.
Nevertheless, this is not an isolated case; it is an experiment that requires asking questions, making observations, and guiding scientific research necessary to eliminate one or more possible hypotheses.
Nature lovers and philosophers focused on ancient texts, particularly on the Latin version of the Bible.
Aristotle's views on all scientific matters, including psychology, were accepted.
As Greek knowledge declined, the West severed its ties with its Greek scientific and philosophical roots.
Many regard physiological and behavioral rhythms as largely arising from endogenous cycles and associate their generation with biological clocks.
Periodic rhythms, which are not merely responses to external periodic cues, have been recorded in most living organisms, including bacteria, fungi, plants, and animals.
Biological clocks are self-sustaining oscillators that maintain a periodic cycle of a free-running rhythm in the absence of external cues.
The Hershey and Chase experiment was one of the most pioneering pieces of evidence indicating the genetic nature of DNA.
"Hershey" and "Chase" used phages or viruses to insert their DNA into bacteria.
They conducted two experiments in which they labeled the DNA in the phage with radioactive phosphorus or the phage protein with radioactive sulfur.
A mutation can have very different effects, depending on the type of mutation, the importance of the genetic segment affected, and whether the affected cells are germ line cells or not.
Only mutations in microbial-like cells can be passed on to children, whereas mutations in other locations may cause cell death or cancer.
Eco-tourism focuses on attracting individuals interested in visiting natural areas, with the aim of enjoying landscapes such as plants and wildlife.
Examples of activities in this area include hunting, fishing, photography, birdwatching, visiting parks, and studying information about the ecosystem.
One example of such an expedition is photography and learning about orchids in Borneo.
Every morning, people from small towns in the county leave by car to go to their workplaces, where they are then transferred by others whose destination is the same place they came from.
In this dynamic transportation shuttle, everyone is connected to and supported by a personal car-based transportation system in some way.
Now science shows that this carbon macroeconomy has taken the biosphere out of one of its stable states—a state that has sustained and supported human evolution over the past two million years.
Everyone in society participates and uses transport systems. Almost everyone has a complaint about the transportation system.
In developed countries, you rarely hear a similar level of complaints about water quality or bridge collapses.
Why have transport systems caused such complaints, why do we witness their failure every day? Is this due to the inefficiency of vehicle engineers, or is there a more fundamental reason?
Traffic flow is the study of the movement of individual drivers and vehicles between two points and their interaction with each other.
Unfortunately, studying traffic flow is difficult, because driver behavior cannot be predicted with 100% certainty.
Fortunately, drivers tend to behave within a suitable and logical range; therefore, traffic flows tend to have a logical structure, which can be approximately specified mathematically.
To better illustrate traffic flow, relationships among the three defined characteristics have been established: 1) flow, 2) density, and 3) speed.
These relationships assist in the planning, design, and operation of road facilities.
Insects were the first group of animals to go into the air. Their ability to fly helped them escape predators more easily and enabled them to find food and mates more effectively.
Most insects have the ability to fold their wings back along their bodies.
This ability enables them to hide from predators across a wider range of smaller spaces.
Today, the only insects that cannot fold their wings behind their backs are midges and one-day mosquitoes.
Thousands of years ago, a man named Aristarchus said that the solar system revolves around the Sun.
Some thought he was right, but many people held a contrary belief—that the solar system, including the Sun (and even other stars), revolved around the Earth.
This seems logical, because on Earth you don't feel like you're moving, right?
The Amazon River is the second longest river in the world and the largest river by volume. The amount of water it carries is eight times greater than that of the world's second-largest river.
The Amazon is also the widest river on Earth, and in some places its width reaches six miles.
Twenty percent of the total water that flows from Earth's rivers into the oceans comes from the Amazon River.
The main channel of the Amazon River is 6,387 kilometers (3,980 miles) long and collects water from thousands of small rivers.
Although the construction of stone pyramids continued until the end of the Old Kingdom, none ever surpassed the Great Pyramids of Giza in size and technical superiority.
The new pharaoh of ancient Egypt was astonished to see the historical structures of their ancestors, which were over a thousand years old.
The population of Vatican City is about 800 people. It is the smallest independent country in the world and also has the smallest population among countries.
The Vatican City uses the Italian language in legislative and administrative communications.
Italian is also a commonly used language by most people working in this state, while Latin is frequently used in religious ceremonies.
All citizens of Vatican City are Roman Catholics.
People have known about fundamental chemical elements such as gold, silver, and copper since ancient times, because all these elements can be found in their pure form in nature, and extracting them using simple early tools is relatively straightforward.
Aristotle, the philosopher, established a hypothesis based on the idea that everything is composed of a combination of one or more of four elements. These elements were earth, water, air, and fire.
It was more similar to the four states of matter (in the same order): solid, liquid, gas, and plasma, although he also proposed the theory that they transform into new forms of matter, which make up what we observe.
Alloys are essentially mixtures of two or more metals. Keep in mind that there are many elements in the periodic table.
Elements such as calcium and potassium are considered metals. Of course, metals like silver and gold also exist.
Some alloys also contain certain amounts of non-metallic elements such as carbon.
Everything in this world is made of matter. All materials are composed of tiny particles called atoms.
Atoms are so incredibly small that trillions of them can fit in the dot at the end of this sentence.
Therefore, when the pencil came out, it became a good friend for many people.
Unfortunately, with the emergence of newer writing methods, pencils are used less and less.
Today, people write their messages on computer screens, and they never have to get near a pencil sharpener.
One can only wonder how the sequence of the sword's blade would be when something never fits together.
The atomic fission bomb is based on the principle that bringing together a nucleus with a large number of protons and neutrons releases energy.
It's somewhat like rolling a heavy cart up a hill. The redivision of an atomic nucleus, followed by the release of some of that energy.
Some atoms have unstable nuclei, meaning they tend to decay either spontaneously or with a slight trigger.
The surface of the Moon is made up of rock and dust. The outer layer of the Moon is called the crust.
The crust is about 70 kilometers thick on the near side and about 100 kilometers thick on the far side.
The crust beneath the ocean is thinner, while the crust beneath highlands is thicker.
As the crust becomes thinner, it may be greater on the side closer to the sea. Magma can rise more easily upward and reach the surface.
Content theories are based on the idea that there are certain things which people repeat or like.
These theories suggest that individuals have specific needs and/or desires that, through growth and the transition to adulthood, have become internalized.
These theories address the question of what causes certain individuals to want to perform the tasks they do, and what factors in their environment lead them to perform or refrain from performing specific tasks.
Two of the most popular content theories are Maslow's hierarchy of needs theory and Herzberg's two-factor theory.
Generally, when managers begin leading their former colleagues, two behaviors emerge. At one end of the spectrum, individuals strive to remain "one of the guys."
This type of manager faces challenges in making unconventional decisions, taking disciplinary actions, evaluating performance, assigning responsibilities, and holding individuals accountable.
At the other end of the spectrum, the individual becomes an indistinguishable person who feels compelled to change everything that the team used to do and to claim it as their own.
Besides these, the leader is ultimately responsible for the team's success and failure.
This behavior often causes rifts between leaders and other team members.
Virtual teams are subject to the same excellence criteria as conventional teams, but there are minor differences in them.
Virtual team members often act as points of contact for their neighboring physical groups.
They often have greater autonomy compared to contract team members because their teams can schedule meetings based on different time zones, but their local management might not be aware of these time zones.
The presence of a "virtual team" (Larson & LaFasto, 1989, p. 109) is also a unique component of a virtual team.
The management team is an "invisible team" to which each member reports. This invisible team establishes specific standards for each member.
Why would an organization want to enter a time-consuming process of establishing an educational organization? One of the objectives is to implement the concepts of innovative organizational learning.
When all available resources are effectively utilized within organizational operational units, creativity and ingenuity can occur.
As a result, the collaborative effort aimed at overcoming obstacles can lead to a new, creative process for meeting customer needs.
Before an organization can become innovative, management must establish a culture of innovation, shared knowledge, and organizational learning.
Farshteh (2006) explains the sequential approach as a method used to help organizations achieve a higher level of performance.
Neuroscientific data provide physical evidence for a theoretical approach to cognition. Therefore, they limit the research domain and make it much more precise.
The relationship between brain pathology and underlying behavior is the subject of scientific research.
It has long been recognized that various types of brain injury, emotional disturbances, trauma, and tumors affect behavior and cause changes in certain mental functions.
The emergence of new technologies enables us to observe and examine brain structures and processes that were previously unseen.
This provides us with a great deal of information and insights to develop simulation models that assist in understanding our mental processes.
Although artificial intelligence is strongly associated with the implicit themes of science fiction, it constitutes a very important field of computer science, dealing with intelligent behavior, learning, and adaptation in machines.
Research in the field of artificial intelligence involves building machines to automate tasks that require intelligent behavior.
Examples include control, planning, scheduling, the ability to respond to customer problems and questions, as well as recognition of handwriting, voice, and face.
Such fields have evolved into distinct disciplines that focus on providing solutions to real-life problems.
Artificial intelligence is now widely used in economic, medical, engineering, and military fields, as well as being embedded in numerous home computers and video game software programs.
A field trip is a major part of any class. Teachers often like to take their students to places that are not accessible by bus.
Technology provides a solution by enabling virtual science trips. Students can view ancient artifacts in museums, visit an aquarium, or appreciate fine arts—all while sitting together with their classmates in the classroom.
Sharing a scientific journey is also an excellent way to reflect on the experience and share insights with future classes.
For example, each year students at Benet School in North Carolina design a website about their trip to the state capital; each year this website is updated, but older versions are kept online to serve as a digital notebook.
Blogs can also help students improve their writing skills. Although students often begin blogging with messy grammar and spelling, the presence of a general audience usually brings about change in this regard.
Since students are often the most critical readers, bloggers strive to improve their writing in order to avoid criticism.
Although blogging "forces students to become more aware of the world around them," it also motivates them to be attentive and interested by requiring them to engage their audience (Toto, 2004).
Blogging is a tool that inspires collaboration and encourages students to extend learning beyond traditional school daily programs.
Proper use of blogs "can help students become more analytical and critical; and by actively responding to online content, students can define their positions within others' writings and also express their views on specific topics" (O’Rourke, 2002).
Ottawa, the attractive and bilingual capital of Canada, boasts a collection of art galleries and museums that showcase Canada's past and present.
Further south are the Niagara Falls, and to the north lie the pristine natural beauty of Muskoka, and beyond that.
All these and other aspects highlight Ontario in the eyes of foreigners as something authentically Canadian.
Large areas in the northern regions are completely sparsely populated, and some are almost entirely uninhabited wilderness areas.
For a demographic comparison that surprises many: the number of African Americans in the United States exceeds that of Canadian citizens in the United States.
The East African Islands are located in the Indian Ocean, off the eastern coast of Africa.
When it comes to wildlife, Madagascar is by far the largest and, in its own right, considered a continent.
Most of the smaller islands are either independent countries or associated with France and are known as luxury holiday destinations.
The Arabs brought Islam to these lands as well, and it took root extensively in the Comoros and Mayotte islands.
European colonization began in the 15th century, and Vasco da Gama, a Portuguese explorer, discovered the Cape route from Europe to India.
To the north, the region is bordered by the desert coast, and to the south and west by the Atlantic Ocean.
Women: It is recommended that female travelers state they are married, regardless of their actual marital status.
Getting a ring is also helpful (not necessarily one that seems expensive).
Women should realize that cultural differences may lead to situations that they find offensive, and it's not unlikely that someone might follow them, grab their hands, and so on.
Be firm in discouraging men and resist without fear (whether there are cultural differences or not, it won't improve the situation!)
The modern city of Casablanca was founded by Berber fishermen in the 10th century BCE, and the Phoenicians, Romans, and Merenids used it as a strategic port, calling it Anfa.
The Portuguese destroyed it and then rebuilt it under the name of Casablanca, and they only abandoned it after the earthquake of 1755.
The Sultan of Morocco reconstructed the city of Dar al-Beyda, and the Spanish merchants who established their trading posts there named it Casablanca.
Casablanca is one of the cities in Morocco whose shopping locations have the least appeal.
Around the old Medina, you can find places selling traditional Moroccan goods, such as tagines, pottery, leather items, hookahs, and a full range of patterns, but all these items are intended solely for tourists.
Goma is a tourist city in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, located in the east and near Rwanda.
In 2002, Goma was destroyed by lava flows from the Nyiragongo volcano, which buried most of the city's streets, especially the city center.
Although security in Goma has been established with accountability, any visit outside Goma should be carefully assessed to gain awareness of the ongoing conflict situation in North Kivu province.
This town also serves as a base for climbing Mount Nyiragongo, along which the most accessible routes to the mountain gorillas in Africa are found.
You can use motorcycle taxis to get around the city of Goma. The usual local price for a short ride is about 500 Congolese francs.
The city of "Timbuktu," which has almost no accessibility, has led its name to be used as a metaphor for strange, remote places.
Today, Timbuktu is a poor town, although due to its historical significance it has tourist appeal and even has an airport.
In 1990, it was added to the list of World Heritage in Danger due to the threat posed by desert sands.
That stop was one of the main stops during Louis Gates' special PBS program titled "The Wonders of the African World."
This city is clearly at odds with other cities in this country, as it is predominantly characterized by Arab traits rather than African ones.
Kruger National Park (KNP) is located in northeastern South Africa, stretching from the eastern border with Mozambique and along the northern border with Zimbabwe, and its southern boundary is the Crocodile River.
This park covers an area of 19,500 square kilometers and is divided into 14 distinct biogeographical realms, each supporting different wildlife.
One of the main attractions of South Africa, and considered the crown jewel of South Africa's national parks (SANParks).
Like all national parks in South Africa, daily conservation and entry to this park are also chargeable.
Purchasing a game card that provides access to selected parks in South Africa or all of South Africa's national parks can be beneficial.
Hong Kong Island has given its name to the Hong Kong Territory and is a place that many tourists consider as their main tourist destination.
The spectacle of the buildings forming Hong Kong's horizon resembles a glowing bar chart, clearly visible alongside the waters of Victoria Harbor.
To get the best view of Hong Kong, leave the island and head toward the opposite shore of Kowloon.
The majority of Hong Kong Island's urban development has been densely concentrated along the northern coastline, on reclaimed land.
This is a place that the British colonists seized for themselves, so if you are looking for evidence of the colonial past of this land, this is a good place to start.
Sundarbans is the largest continuous mangrove forest in the world, stretching 80 kilometers (50 miles) from the coasts of Bangladesh and India inland.
"Sundarbans" has been declared a UNESCO World Heritage site. The forest area located within the territory of India is called Sundarbans Forest Park.
These forests are not just the Karna swamps—they also contain some of the last remaining stretches of vast forests that once covered the Sindh and Ganges plains.
The Sundarbans area covers approximately 3,850 square kilometers, with about one-third of it consisting of water and swamps.
Sundarbans has been a wildlife sanctuary since 1966, and it is estimated that currently 400 royal Bengal tigers and about 30,000 deer have been sighted in this region.
Buses depart throughout the day from the inter-district station (opposite the river), but most of them, especially those heading east and to Jakaar/Bumteng, operate between 6:30 and 7:30 a.m.
Since intercity buses are often full, it is recommended to book tickets a few days in advance.
Most areas are covered by small, comfortable, and sturdy Japanese ferries.
Shared taxis are one of the fast and convenient ways to travel to nearby locations such as Paro (Nu 150) and Punakha (Nu 200).
The Oiapoque River Bridge is a cable bridge. This bridge is built over the Oiapoque River to connect the cities of Oiapoque in Brazil and Saint-Georges de l'Oyapock in French Guiana.
The height of the two towers is 83 meters, the length is 378 meters, and it has two lanes with a width of 3.50 meters.
The vertical clearance under the bridge is 15 meters. Its construction was completed in August 2011, and it was opened for use in March 2017.
The bridge is scheduled for completion in September 2017, which is the expected time for completing the customs stations.
The Guarani were the most important indigenous group inhabiting present-day Eastern Paraguay, living as semi-nomadic hunters and also engaging in subsistence agriculture.
The Chaco region was also home to other indigenous groups such as the Guaycurú and Payaguá, who sustained themselves through hunting, gathering, and fishing.
In the 16th century, Paraguay, which had previously been known as the "Great Land of the Indians," came into existence as a result of the encounter between Spanish conquistadors and native groups.
The Spaniards initiated the colonial period, which lasted three centuries.
Paraguay has managed to preserve many of its indigenous characteristics and traits since the founding of Asunción in 1537.
Argentina is renowned for having one of the best rugby teams and the best players in the world.
The biggest event of the year takes place in December at the "Las Canitas" polo fields.
Smaller tournaments and events can also be seen here at other times of the year.
For information about tournament news and ticket purchase locations for the polo tournaments, please visit Asociacion Argentina de Polo.
The official currency of the Falkland Islands is the Falkland pound (FKP), which is set at an equivalent value of one British pound (GBP).
Currency exchange is available at the only bank on the islands, located in Stanley, along with the FIC West shop.
The British pound is usually accepted throughout the islands, and Visa and American dollar cards are also generally accepted.
Credit cards may not be accepted in more remote islands, although British and U.S. currency is accepted; please arrange the accepted payment method in advance with the owners.
It is almost impossible to exchange Falkland Islands currency outside the islands, so exchange your money before leaving the islands.
Since Montevideo is located south of the equator, when it is winter in the Northern Hemisphere, it is summer there, and vice versa.
Montevideo is a semi-tropical region; during the summer months, temperatures usually rise above 30 degrees Celsius.
Winter can be deceptively cold: temperatures rarely drop below freezing, but wind and humidity combine to make you feel colder than the thermometer indicates.
There is no distinct dry or rainy season: rainfall remains almost constant throughout the year.
Although many animals in the park are accustomed to seeing humans, wildlife remains wild and should not be fed or disturbed.
According to park authorities, keep a distance of at least 100 meters from bears and wolves, and 25 meters from other wild animals!
American bison, elk, moose, bears, and nearly all large animals, no matter how tame they may seem, can attack.
Every year, dozens of visitors are injured due to not maintaining an appropriate distance. These are large, wild, and inherently dangerous animals, so do not enter their territory.
In addition, keep in mind that odors attract bears and other wild animals, so avoid carrying or cooking fragrant foods, and keep your campsite clean.
Apia is the capital city of Samoa. This town is located on the island of Upolu and has a population of slightly less than 40,000 people.
Apia was established in the 1850s and has been the official capital of Samoa since 1959.
This port is famous for the blockade of the fleet in 1889, when seven ships from Germany, the United States, and Britain did not leave the port.
Except for one British warship, all the ships sank, and nearly 200 American and German people lost their lives.
During an attempt to achieve independence under the organization of the Maoist movement, a peaceful gathering in the town led to the killing of the chief leader "Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III."
Due to Auckland's location between two harbors, there are many beaches. The most popular ones are located in three areas.
The North Shore (in the North Harbour area) lies in the Pacific Ocean and stretches from Long Beach in the north to Donport in the south.
They are almost all sandy beaches where swimming is safe, and most of them have shade from Pohutukawa trees.
The Tamaki Drive beaches in the Waitemata Harbour are located in the suburbs of the renowned cities of Mission Bay and Saint Heliers in central Auckland.
These are family beaches, sometimes busy, with good shops along the shoreline. Swimming is safe.
The main local beer is called "Number One," which is not a complex beer, but is pleasant and refreshing. The other local beer is called "Manta."
There are many French wines available for consumption, but wines from New Zealand and Australia may be better preserved during transport.
Drinking local tap water is completely safe, but if you are concerned, you can easily find bottled water.
For Australians, "flat white" is an unfamiliar concept. A short black is the same as an espresso, a cappuccino is served with a generous amount of foam (and not just the crema), and tea is served without milk.
The hot chocolate meets Belgian standards. The fruit juices are expensive but excellent.
Many trips take place throughout the year to coral reefs, and injuries resulting from these factors at coral reefs are rare.
However, listen to the officials' advice, follow the signs, and pay close attention to the safety warnings.
Sea angels in boxes are found near the coast and near the mouths of rivers in the north of 1770 from October to April. Occasionally, they can be found outside of these periods.
Sharks exist, but they rarely attack humans. Most sharks fear humans and move away.
Saltwater crocodiles do not actively live in the ocean; their primary habitat is the river delta in northern Rockhampton.
Booking in advance gives travelers peace of mind that they will have a place to stay when they arrive at their destination.
Travel agencies often have contracts with specific hotels; although, through a travel agency, you may also find information about other types of accommodation, such as campgrounds.
Travel agencies typically offer packages that include breakfast, airport transfer arrangements, or even combined flight and hotel packages.
They can also reserve time for you if you need additional time to consider a proposal or prepare other documents for your destination (such as a visa).
Therefore, any amendment or request must be made through the travel agency agent and not directly through the hotel.
In some music festivals, a large portion of attendees decide to camp on-site, and most of these attendees consider this camping aspect an essential part of the event.
If you want to be close to the stage, you need to arrive early to secure a camping spot near the music performance area.
Please keep in mind that although music may have ended in the main performance areas, some parts of the festival might continue playing music until late at night.
Some festivals have special areas designated for families with young children to set up camp.
If you cross the northern Baltic Sea in winter, check the cabin area, because as you pass over the ice, relatively terrifying sounds can be heard by those more susceptible.
City tourism in St. Petersburg involves spending time in the city. Cruise travelers are exempt from visa requirements (please check the regulations).
Casinos usually make great efforts to maximize the time and money that guests spend. Windows and clocks are usually absent, and exit doors are difficult to find.
They usually provide special food, beverages, and entertainment to keep guests happy and make them stay at the place.
Some venues serve alcoholic beverages. However, intoxication impairs judgment, and all good gamblers understand the importance of alertness.
Anyone planning to drive at high altitudes or through mountain passes should take into account the possibility of snow, ice, or freezing temperatures.
On icy and snowy roads, traction is low, so you cannot drive as you would on solid asphalt.
During a snowstorm, the snow can suddenly fall so heavily that it catches you off guard.
Also, visibility may be reduced due to snowfall or blowing snow, or due to condensation or ice buildup on the vehicle's windows.
On the other hand, icy and snowy conditions are common in many countries, and traffic usually proceeds throughout the year without interruption.
Safaris might be considered the biggest tourism attraction in Africa, which is fascinating for many visitors.
The term "safari" generally refers to a land journey for viewing the amazing wildlife of Africa, particularly in the savanna.
Some animals, such as elephants and giraffes, tend to approach cars, and standard equipment provides suitable opportunities for viewing them.
Lions, bears, and tigers sometimes become shy, and you can see them better with a hunting camera.
A safari is a walk (also called "leaf and branch walking," "safari walking," or "walking") that involves walking for several hours or days.
The Paralympic Games will take place from August 24 to September 5, 2021. Some of these competitions will be held in locations across Japan.
Tokyo will be the only Asian city to have hosted two Summer Olympics, and it hosted these games in 1964.
If you have already booked your flight and accommodation for 2020 before the delay was announced, you might be in a dire situation.
Cancellation policies vary, but by the end of March, most cancellation policies based on the coronavirus will not continue beyond July 2020, which is the time set for the Olympics.
It is expected that most event tickets will be priced between 2,500 and 130,000 yen, with the average ticket cost around 7,000 yen.
Ironing damp clothes can help dry them. Many hotels provide irons and ironing boards for loan, even if they are not in the room.
If an iron is not available or if you do not feel like wearing ironed socks, you may use a hairdryer if accessible.
Be careful not to let the fabric become too hot (as it could cause bleeding, or in a worse case, burns).
There are various methods for water treatment, some of which are more effective against specific threats.
In some areas, boiling water for one minute is sufficient, and in others, it needs to be boiled for a few minutes.
Filters vary in effectiveness, and if you're concerned about this, you should consider purchasing bottled drinking water from a reputable company.
Travelers may encounter various harmful animals with which they are unfamiliar in their native areas.
Pests spoil food, cause annoyance and discomfort, and in worse cases trigger allergic reactions, spread their own poison, or transmit infections.
Infectious diseases or dangerous animals that can injure or kill people with their strength are usually not described as disasters.
Shopping at duty-free stores offers the opportunity to purchase goods without paying taxes and customs duties in certain locations.
Travel restrictions for countries with heavy taxes can sometimes lead to significant savings, especially regarding products such as alcoholic beverages and tobacco products.
The stretch between Point Marion and Fermont features the most challenging driving conditions on the Buffalo Expressway, as it frequently passes through scattered isolated forest lands.
If you're not accustomed to driving on rural roads, be extra careful: there are more steep slopes, narrow lanes, and sharp turns.
The posted speed limits are significantly lower than those in the preceding and following sections—typically 35 to 40 miles per hour (56 to 64 kilometers per hour)—and observing them carefully is even more important than in other sections.
To my great surprise, mobile phone services here are much stronger than in other parts of the route, such as Pennsylvania Wilds.
German pastries are excellent and in Bavaria they are diverse and rich, mostly resembling what is cooked in their southern neighbor, Austria.
Fruit desserts are common. Apple desserts are baked throughout the year, and in summer, cherries and plums are used for decoration.
Many German desserts contain almonds, walnuts, and other tree nuts. Popular cakes are often served with a cup of strong coffee.
If you're looking for small but filling pastries, simply try something known as Berliner, Pfannkuchen, or Krapfen, depending on your region.
A kari is a dish prepared with plants and spices, along with meat or vegetables.
Depending on the amount of liquid present, a task can be "dry" or "wet".
In central northern India and Pakistan, yogurt is typically used in kari dishes; in southern India and some coastal regions of the subcontinent, coconut milk is usually used.
Indonesian cuisine is a broad term encompassing a wide diversity of regional cooking across this nation, as Indonesia offers the ability to choose from among 17,000 islands.
However, in the absence of using additional qualifications, this term originally means "food" and is derived from the central and eastern parts of the main island of Java.
Javanese cuisine, which is now widely available throughout the Indonesian archipelago, features a range of simple, flavorful dishes, with dominant Javanese seasonings being peanuts, chili, sugar (especially Javanese coconut sugar), and various aromatic spices.
The stirrup is a support for the rider's foot, hanging from both sides of the saddle.
Although they provide greater stability for the rider, they may pose safety concerns because the rider's feet could get caught in them.
If a rider falls off a horse and his foot gets caught in the stirrup, the horse will be dragged along the ground if it runs away. To minimize this risk, many precautionary measures can be taken.
First of all, most motorcyclists wear riding boots that have a heel and a narrow, pointed toe.
In the next step, some saddles, especially English saddles, have safety bars that allow the girth leather to detach from the saddle in case the horse rider falls.
In Cochamó—Chile's premier mountaineering destination, known as South America's Yosemite—there are various types of granite walls and cliffs.
From the highest point, one can enjoy breathtaking views of the peaks. Mountaineers continuously create new routes among numerous paths and potentially sheer cliffs from all corners of the world.
Winter sports on snow, such as skiing and snowboarding, are popular activities in which, using ski poles or a snowboard attached to your feet, you glide down snowy, sloped terrain.
Skiing is the main activity of the trip, which has many enthusiasts and is sometimes known as "ski tourism," meaning planning the entire holiday around skiing at a specific location.
The idea of skiing is very old — cave paintings depicting skiers date back to 5000 BC.
Skiing on hills dates back to at least the 17th century, and in 1861, the Norwegians established the first recreational ski club in Australia.
Skiing trip: This activity is also called skiing in remote areas, ski tour, or ski trekking.
This is related to it, but usually does not include ski tours or alpine-style mountaineering; the latter is carried out on steep terrain and requires more sturdy skis and crampons.
Just as you think about the walking path, think about the ski trail as well.
Under good weather conditions, you can cover much greater distances compared to walking—but it's very rare that you can reach cross-country skiing speeds without carrying a heavy backpack on well-maintained trails.
Europe is a relatively small continent, yet it contains numerous independent countries. Under normal circumstances, traveling between multiple countries requires going through visa application processes and passport control several times.
Nevertheless, the Schengen area functions like a country in this respect.
As long as you are residing in this region, you can generally cross the borders without having to go through the passport checkpoint again.
Similarly, with a Schengen visa, you no longer need to apply for a visa separately for each Schengen member country, thus saving you time, money, and paperwork.
There is no universal definition for considering manufactured items as antiques. Some tax authorities consider items older than 100 years to be antiques.
This definition varies geographically, particularly in regions such as North America, where the age restriction may be lower than in Europe.
Handicraft products may be defined as antiques, although they are newer than similar mass-produced items.
Rearing Arctic deer is an important livelihood among the Sami, and the cultural exchange surrounding that trade is also significant for many other professions.
Although traditionally not all Semites were involved in raising large goats, they lived by fishing, hunting, and similar activities, and used goats mostly as working animals.
Today, many Sami people work in modern commerce. Tourism is one of the important sources of income in Lappland, the Sami region.
Although widely used, especially among non-Romani people, the word "kuli" is often considered offensive due to its association with negative stereotypes and misconceptions about Romani people.
If the country you are visiting is subject to a travel advisory, your travel health insurance or trip cancellation insurance may be affected.
You might also want to use recommendations from other governments besides your own, but those recommendations are designed for their own citizens.
For example, American citizens in the Middle East may encounter different conditions than Europeans or Arabs.
Warnings are merely a brief summary of a country's political situation.
The opinions expressed, in comparison with more detailed information available elsewhere, are often superficial, general, and very simple.
Harsh weather conditions refer to a general term for dangerous weather phenomena that may cause damage, disrupt social life, or result in loss of human lives.
Dry climates can occur anywhere on Earth, and there are various types of them, which may depend on geography, topography, and weather conditions.
Strong winds, hail, excessive rainfall, fires, as well as lightning, tornadoes, waterfalls, and seasonal storms are all forms and effects of severe weather.
Regional and seasonal extreme weather phenomena include blizzards, snowstorms, ice storms, and dust storms.
Travelers are strongly advised to be aware of any potential severe weather conditions affecting their area, as these conditions may impact travel plans.
Anyone intending to visit a country considered a war zone must undergo professional training.
Searching the internet for the phrase 'hostile environment training' will likely show the address of a local company.
A training course usually covers all the issues raised here in much greater detail and typically includes practical experience as well.
A training course usually lasts 2 to 5 days and includes role-playing, a high volume of first aid training, and sometimes weapon use training.
There are many books and magazines dealing with survival in the wild, but publications focusing on war zones are few and far between.
Travelers planning to undergo gender transition abroad should carry valid documents for return travel.
Governments' willingness to issue gender-neutral (X) passports or update documents to match individuals' preferred names and genders varies.
Foreign governments' willingness to authenticate these documents varies significantly.
Security checks at checkpoints have also become much stricter since September 11, 2001.
Transgender individuals should not expect to pass through scanners while preserving their privacy and dignity before surgery.
Breaking waves are the backwash currents of waves that move from the shore toward the sea, which usually occur at cliffs or similar locations.
The return current is focused in several deeper sections due to underwater topography, and a fast flow toward deeper water may occur there.
Most drownings occur due to exhaustion from struggling to swim against the wave's current, which can be impossible.
As soon as you exit the current, swimming back will not be any harder than usual.
Try to aim for a spot where you won't get stuck again, or, depending on your skills and whether they've noticed your presence or not, you might be able to wait for the rescue operation.
The shock of returning home occurs earlier than cultural shock (it happens earlier than the honeymoon phase), lasts longer, and can be more intense.
Travelers who have easily adapted to a new culture sometimes find it very difficult to return to their own native culture.
Upon returning home after living abroad, you have adopted a new culture and have lost some of your habits from your native culture.
At the beginning of their foreign travel, people were likely patient and understanding, knowing that travelers arriving in a new country need time to adapt.
People might not expect that patience and understanding of the circumstances are essential for travelers returning home.
The sound of the pyramid and the light show is one of the most fascinating things for children in this area.
You can see the pyramids in the dark, and you can observe them in silence before the show begins.
You can always hear the sounds of tourists and vendors. The sound and light show is just like a storybook.
Sphinx stands as the narrator and behind-the-scenes figure of a long story.
These scenes are projected onto the pyramids, and different pyramids are illuminated.
South Shetland Islands, discovered in 1819, are claimed by many countries and, with sixteen active bases in 2020, have the highest number of bases.
This archipelago is located 120 kilometers north of the peninsula. Its largest island is King George, and its inhabited area is called Villa Las Estrellas (Star Village).
Other islands include Livingstone and Despension, which features a volcanic lake atop an active volcano, providing a spectacular natural harbor.
Althorland is a region in the southern part of the peninsula, bounded by the Bellinghausen Sea.
The peninsula's mountains merge with the plateau here, then rise again to form a 360-kilometer-long range of the Sawtooth Mountains, which the Minnesota Glacier has split into two.
The northern part or the Sentinel Range contains the highest mountain in the South Pole, the Vinson Massif, which has an elevation of 4,892 meters at Mount Vinson.
In remote areas, without mobile phone coverage, a satellite phone may be your only option.
A satellite phone is generally not a substitute for a mobile phone, because to make a phone call, you must be outdoors and have a clear view of the satellite.
